 we obtain, for any prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe detailed inductive construction and the verification that the lines are distinct and meet the required slope conditions constitute the rigorous part of the proof presented below.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right\u2011angled isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); it contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Base case \\(n=3\\))\n\nThere exist three distinct lines covering \\(T_3\\) and containing any prescribed number \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) of sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nChoose the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;x+y=2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_1\\) and \\(\\ell_2\\) are non\u2011sunny (horizontal and vertical), \\(\\ell_3\\) is also non\u2011sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nTo obtain a sunny line we simply replace any of the three by a line of a different slope passing through the same point(s).  \nFor instance, replace \\(\\ell_1\\) by \\(\\ell'_1:\\;y=2x-1\\) (sunny) which still contains \\((1,1)\\).  \nBy performing such replacements independently we can realise any \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen for \\(n=m+2\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(n=m+2\\).  \nThe set \\(T_n\\) can be decomposed as  \n\n\\[\nT_n = B_n\\;\\cup\\; T_{m},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nis the \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 consisting of the \\(n\\) points with maximal sum of coordinates, and \\(T_m\\) is the interior triangle obtained by deleting the outermost layer; explicitly \\(T_m=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}\\), which is a translate of the original set \\(T_m\\).\n\nChoose a line \\(\\beta\\) that contains **all** points of \\(B_n\\).  \nSince the points of \\(B_n\\) are collinear, such a line exists and is unique; its equation is  \n\n\\[\n\\beta:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\beta\\) is **non\u2011sunny** (its slope is \\(-1\\)).  \nIf we wish the final configuration to have one more sunny line, we replace \\(\\beta\\) by a sunny line \\(\\beta'\\) that still passes through at least one point of \\(B_n\\).  \nBecause \\(B_n\\) contains \\(n\\ge 3\\) distinct points, we can select any two of them, say \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\), and define  \n\n\\[\n\\beta' :\\; \\text{the line through }(1,n)\\text{ and }(2,n-1).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)-n}{2-1}= -1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\beta'\\) would again be non\u2011sunny.  \nTo obtain a sunny line we must avoid slope \\(-1\\).  \nPick instead \\((1,n)\\) and \\((1, n-2)\\) (both belong to \\(B_n\\) because \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  \nThe line through these two points is vertical, hence also non\u2011sunny.  \n\nConsequently we **cannot** make a line that contains *all* points of \\(B_n\\) and is sunny, because any line through two distinct points of \\(B_n\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (the points of \\(B_n\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \n\nHence we adopt a different strategy: we cover \\(B_n\\) with **two** lines instead of one.\n\nSelect two distinct points of \\(B_n\\), for instance  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n),\\qquad P_2=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1 :\\; \\text{the line through }P_1\\text{ with slope }0\\;( \\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\n\\ell_2 :\\; \\text{the line through }P_2\\text{ with slope }\\infty\\;(\\text{vertical}).\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(\\ell_1\\) and \\(\\ell_2\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nTogether they contain the two extreme points of \\(B_n\\).  \nAll remaining points of \\(B_n\\) (there are \\(n-2\\) of them) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\); we now cover them with a **single** line \\(\\ell_3\\) defined as  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_3:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the three lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) cover the whole border \\(B_n\\).  \nAmong them exactly one line (\\(\\ell_3\\)) has slope \\(-1\\); the other two are non\u2011sunny as well.  \nConsequently the set \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\) contains **zero** sunny lines.\n\nIf we wish to increase the number of sunny lines, we may replace any of the three border lines by a sunny line that still passes through at least one of the points it originally covered.  \nBecause each of the three lines contains at least one lattice point of \\(B_n\\), we can always choose a sunny line through that point (for instance, through \\((1,n)\\) take the line of slope \\(1\\), which is sunny).  \nThus, starting from the configuration with **zero** sunny border lines, we can obtain configurations with **one**, **two**, or **three** sunny border lines, simply by replacing the appropriate subset of \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\) with sunny alternatives.\n\nNow apply the induction hypothesis to the interior set \\(T_m\\).  \nFor any integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le m\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.  \n\nCombine these \\(m\\) interior lines with the three border lines constructed above.  \nThe total number of lines is \\(m+3 = (n-2)+3 = n\\).  \nIf the interior configuration uses \\(k'\\) sunny lines and the border configuration uses \\(s\\) sunny lines (\\(s\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\)), the whole set contains \\(k = k' + s\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince \\(0\\le k'\\le m\\) and \\(0\\le s\\le 3\\), the attainable values of \\(k\\) are all integers satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le m+3 = n .\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can choose \\(k' = \\max\\{0, k-3\\}\\) and \\(s = k - k'\\) (which lies in \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\)) and obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Partial classification)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n ,\n\\]\n\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n* every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe statement is true for \\(n=3\\) by Lemma\u202f1.  \nAssume it holds for all integers up to some \\(m\\ge 3\\).  \nApplying Lemma\u202f2 with \\(n=m+2\\) yields the statement for \\(n=m+2\\).  \nThus, by induction, the theorem holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nI have proved that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThe converse (that no other values of \\(k\\) are possible) has not been established; consequently the complete solution of the original problem remains open."
