order lines are all **non‑sunny**.  \n   To increase the number of sunny lines we may replace any of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) by a *sunny* line that still passes through a point of \\(B_n\\) that it originally covered.  \n   For example, replace \\(\\ell_1\\) (which contains \\((1,n)\\)) by the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell'_1:\\;y=n+(x-1) \\quad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, sunny)},\n   \\]\n\n   or replace \\(\\ell_2\\) (which contains \\((n,1)\\)) by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell'_2:\\;x=n+(y-1) \\quad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, sunny)}.\n   \\]\n\n   Replacing \\(\\ell_3\\) is also possible: choose any two distinct points of \\(B_n\\) that are not collinear with slope \\(-1\\) (e.g. \\((1,n)\\) and \\((1,n-2)\\)) and take the line through them; its slope is \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) (non‑sunny) – to obtain a sunny line we instead take a line through a single point of \\(B_n\\) with any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Because each replacement uses a different slope, the resulting lines remain distinct.\n\n   Consequently, by independently deciding for each of the three border lines whether to keep it non‑sunny or to replace it by a sunny line, we can obtain **any** number  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\n   \\]\n\n   of sunny border lines.\n\n   Adding the interior configuration with \\(k'\\) sunny lines yields a total of  \n\n   \\[\n   k=k'+s\\qquad (0\\le k'\\le m,\\;0\\le s\\le 3).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(m=n-2\\), the attainable values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le (n-2)+3=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can construct a family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Upper bound.**  \n   A family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, so at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny. Hence no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   By induction the construction works for all \\(n\\ge 3\\); together with the trivial upper bound we obtain the exact set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\\exists\\;\\text{a family }\\mathcal L\\text{ of }n\\text{ distinct lines}\n               \\text{ covering }T_n\\text{ and containing exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nConsider the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;x+y=2 .\n\\]\n\nAll three are non‑sunny.  \nTo obtain a sunny line we may replace any \\(\\ell_i\\) by a line of a different slope passing through a point already on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nFor instance, replace \\(\\ell_1\\) by \\(\\ell'_1:\\;y=2x-1\\) (slope \\(2\\), sunny) which still contains \\((1,1)\\).  \n\nBecause the three points are distinct, we can perform such replacements independently, thereby obtaining families with any prescribed number \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) of sunny lines.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_3=\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some integer \\(m\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_m=\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWe prove that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_{m+2}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,m+2\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(n=m+2\\).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n    =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\},\n\\]\nthe “border’’ of the triangle, and  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a translate of the original set \\(T_m\\).  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\nT_n = B_n\\;\\cup\\; T_{m},\n\\qquad B_n\\cap T_{m}=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1  Covering the border  \n\nDefine three distinct lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y=n \\qquad\\text{(horizontal, slope }0),\\\\[2pt]\n\\ell_2 &: x=n \\qquad\\text{(vertical, slope }\\infty),\\\\[2pt]\n\\ell_3 &: x+y=n+1 \\qquad\\text{(slope }-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n* \\(\\ell_1\\) contains the point \\((1,n)\\in B_n\\).  \n* \\(\\ell_2\\) contains the point \\((n,1)\\in B_n\\).  \n* Every remaining point of \\(B_n\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_3\\).\n\nThus the three lines together cover the whole border \\(B_n\\).  \nAll three are **non‑sunny**.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.2  Turning border lines sunny  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\) we may either keep \\(\\ell_i\\) as it is (non‑sunny) or replace it by a sunny line that still passes through a point of \\(B_n\\) that \\(\\ell_i\\) originally covered.\n\n*Replacement of \\(\\ell_1\\).*  \n\\(\\ell_1\\) contains \\((1,n)\\). Choose any slope \\(\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (e.g. \\(\\sigma=1\\)) and define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell'_1:\\; y-n = \\sigma\\,(x-1).\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell'_1\\) passes through \\((1,n)\\) and is sunny.  \n\n*Replacement of \\(\\ell_2\\).*  \nAnalogously, using the point \\((n,1)\\) we set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell'_2:\\; x-n = \\sigma\\,(y-1),\\qquad \\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Replacement of \\(\\ell_3\\).*  \nPick any single point of \\(B_n\\), say \\((1,n)\\), and draw a line through it with a sunny slope \\(\\sigma\\). Call it \\(\\ell'_3\\).  \n\nBecause the three original lines intersect the border at three *different* points, the three replacements (if performed) have distinct slopes and therefore remain distinct lines.  \n\nConsequently, by independently deciding for each of the three border lines whether to keep it or to replace it, we can obtain **any** number  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\n\\]\n\nof sunny border lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.3  Covering the interior  \n\nBy the induction hypothesis (3) there exists, for every integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le m\\), a family \\(\\mathcal I_{k'}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines that covers the interior set \\(T_m\\) and contains exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.4  Assembling the whole family  \n\nTake the \\(m\\) interior lines \\(\\mathcal I_{k'}\\) and the three border lines (after the chosen replacements).  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nm+3 = (n-2)+3 = n .\n\\]\n\nThe total number of sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\nk = k' + s ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(k'\\in\\{0,\\dots ,m\\}\\) and \\(s\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \nSince \\(m=n-2\\), the possible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le (n-2)+3 = n .\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Upper bound  \n\nA family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, so at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny. Hence no \\(k>n\\) can belong to \\(\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nBy the base case \\(n=3\\) and the inductive step proved above, (4) holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nTogether with the trivial upper bound we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal K_n=\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, **every** integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) (inclusive) can occur as the number of sunny lines, and no other value is possible. ∎"
