onst}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nConsider the set  \n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) the vertical line \\(x=t\\) contains exactly the points \\((t,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\); similarly, the horizontal line \\(y=t\\) contains the points \\((a,t)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\).  \n\nBecause a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point having a given \\(y\\)-coordinate, a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point having a given \\(x\\)-coordinate, and a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point having a given value of \\(a-b\\), the following holds:\n\n*If a line is sunny, then for each fixed value of \\(a\\) (or of \\(b\\) or of \\(a-b\\)) it can contain **at most one** point of \\(S\\).*\n\nNow the set \\(S\\) contains the \\(n\\) distinct points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\); they all have different \\(x\\)-coordinates. To cover these \\(n\\) points with sunny lines, we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, because a single sunny line can contain at most one of them. Since we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines in total, every line would have to be used for exactly one of these points, leaving no line available to cover any other point of \\(S\\). This is impossible because \\(S\\) contains many more points (in fact \\(|S|=n(n+1)/2>n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\)). Hence the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny leads to a contradiction.\n\nTherefore any admissible configuration must contain at least one non‑sunny line, and the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThese two observations give the partial result  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-1},\n\\]\n\nwith the extremal value \\(k=0\\) known to be attainable. Determining which intermediate values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) can actually occur remains open.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
