            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least two lines are non‑sunny\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that at most one of the \\(n\\) lines is non‑sunny.  \nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S\\) having a prescribed\n\\(x\\)-coordinate, because two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate would lie on a vertical line, which is forbidden.  \n\nHence the \\(n\\) lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinates are distinct.  \nBut \\(S\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) with distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, and also many other points (e.g. \\((1,2)\\)).  After using the \\(n\\) lines to cover those \\(n\\) points, no line remains to cover the remaining \\(|S|-n>0\\) points, a contradiction.  \n\nTherefore **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, and\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The lines\n\n*Two non‑sunny lines*  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad H:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines* – slope \\(1\\).  For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + (k+1-j).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\{V,H,L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nare distinct: \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) are perpendicular, and the \\(L_j\\) have different\nintercepts \\(k+1-j\\).\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\); this slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\nundefined (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)), so every \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nIf \\(n-k>2\\) we may add any \\(n-k-2\\) further vertical lines (e.g. \\(x=2, x=3,\\dots\\));\nthey are non‑sunny and do not affect the covering argument.  Hence we obtain a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n#### 2.2  Covering all points of \\(S\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(V\\).  \n*If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(H\\).\n\nAssume now \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).  \nWrite \\(a=1+r\\) with \\(r\\ge1\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n-r .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-2\\), inequality (9) implies \\(b\\le k+2\\).\n\nIf \\(r\\ge k+1\\) then \\(a\\ge k+2\\) and (9) gives \\(b\\le n-(k+1)\\le1\\), contradicting \\(b\\ge2\\).  \nHence \\(1\\le r\\le k\\); set \\(j=r\\) (so \\(1\\le j\\le k\\)).\n\nFor this \\(j\\) the line \\(L_j\\) from (7) has equation  \n\n\\[\nb = a + (k+1-j).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(j=r=a-1\\), the right‑hand side of (10) equals  \n\n\\[\na + (k+1-(a-1)) = k+2 .\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) we have \\(b\\le k+2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\); the only integer \\(b\\) satisfying (10) is precisely \\(b = a + (k+1-j)\\).  Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) belongs to one of the lines in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe argument of §1 shows that at least two lines must be non‑sunny, giving the\nnecessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction of §2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
