sely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny.\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(S\\) lies on one of the lines.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   - If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(V\\).  \n   - If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(H\\).\n\n   Assume now \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  Write \\(a=1+r\\) with \\(r\\ge 1\\).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a = n-r .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\le n-2\\), inequality (3) yields \\(b\\le k+2\\).\n\n   If \\(r\\ge k+1\\) then \\(a\\ge k+2\\) and (3) gives \\(b\\le n-(k+1)\\le 1\\), contradicting \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n   Hence \\(1\\le r\\le k\\).  Set \\(j=r\\) (so \\(1\\le j\\le k\\)).  For this \\(j\\) the line \\(L_j\\) from (2) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   b = a + (k+1-j).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(j=r=a-1\\), the right\u2011hand side of (4) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   a + (k+1-(a-1)) = k+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   From (3) we have \\(b\\le k+2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\); the only integer \\(b\\) satisfying (4) is precisely the given \\(b\\).  Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_j\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(S\\) belongs to one of the lines in the family, and the family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Conclusion** \u2013 the argument in (1) shows that at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny, giving the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); the explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be realized.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"

