"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Trivial upper bound.**  \n   Since we have only \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny; therefore \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   Keep the horizontal lines  \n   \\[\n      H_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n   They are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line of slope \\(1\\)  \n   \\[\n      L_j:\\; y = x + c_j,\\qquad c_j:=n-2k+j .\n   \\]\n   Because the slope is \\(1\\), none of the \\(L_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently all \\(L_j\\) are sunny.\n\n   *Coverage proof.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   - If \\(b\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n   - If \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) set \\(d:=b-a\\). From the inequalities \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n     \\[\n        n-2k+1\\le d\\le n-k .\n     \\]\n     Indeed,\n     \\[\n        d=b-a\\ge (n-k+1)- (n+1-b)=2b-n-1\\ge n-2k+1,\n     \\]\n     and\n     \\[\n        d=b-a\\le b-1\\le n-k .\n     \\]\n     Hence \\(d\\) equals one of the numbers \\(c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a+c_j\\) for the appropriate \\(j\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(L_j\\).\n\n   Thus every required point belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines. The \\(n\\) lines are distinct because the horizontal lines have distinct \\(y\\)-intercepts and the sunny lines have distinct intercepts \\(c_j\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); therefore all these \\(k\\) are attainable. Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) this yields the exact set of admissible values.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions:\nthe \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, and the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nWe are asked for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines among a collection of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines.  Obviously a collection of \\(n\\) lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering\ncondition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1. The non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_i\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore it is **not** sunny.\n\n#### 2.2. The sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + c_j,\\qquad c_j:=n-2k+j .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_j\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently they are not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-k}\\cup\\{L_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) are distinct because\nthe \\(H_i\\) have different \\(y\\)-intercepts and the \\(L_j\\) have different\nintercepts \\(c_j\\).\n\n#### 2.3. Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1:* \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) from (2).\n\n*Case 2:* \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  \nSet \\(d:=b-a\\).  From \\(a\\ge1\\) we have \\(d\\le b-1\\le n-k\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nd=b-a\\ge b-(n+1-b)=2b-n-1\\ge n-2k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1\\le d\\le n-k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand side of (4) is exactly the set \\(\\{c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\) defined in (3).  Therefore there exists a unique \\(j\\) with \\(c_j=d\\), and the point satisfies \\(b=a+c_j\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n#### 2.4. Counting sunny lines\n\nAmong the \\(n\\) lines, precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) have slope\n\\(1\\) and are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are not sunny.\nHence the number of sunny lines in this configuration equals the prescribed\n\\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completion of the proof\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* By (1) any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(k\\le n\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the construction above provides\n  \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a\nconfiguration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
