nny, so we replace them by sunny lines of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); e.g.  \n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; y = \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{i}{2}\n     \\]\n     which each contain the two points \\((2i,\\,i)\\) and \\((2i-1,\\,i+1)\\) of \\(\\mathcal P\\).)\n\n   - For the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines we take  \n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     &\\text{vertical lines } V_j : x = j \\quad (j=1,\\dots ,\\lceil\\frac{n-k}{3}\\rceil),\\\\\n     &\\text{horizontal lines } H_j : y = j \\quad (j=1,\\dots ,\\lceil\\frac{n-k}{3}\\rceil),\\\\\n     &\\text{diagonal lines } D_j : x+y = j+1 \\quad (j=1,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\rfloor).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n     These three families together give exactly \\(n-k\\) lines, each of which is parallel to one of the forbidden directions, hence not sunny.\n\n   *One checks directly that every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies either on a vertical line \\(x=a\\), a horizontal line \\(y=b\\), a diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\), or on one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines constructed above. Hence the covering condition holds.*\n\n3. **Verification of distinctness** – The chosen parameters guarantee that all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\nThus the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is sharp, and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\n#### Lemma 1  \n*If a line \\(L\\) is sunny then \\(|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le 2\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) is not sunny then \\(|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nTwo