((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies one of the three equations\n   above, hence \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Moreover each point\n   belongs to at least three lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) (vertical, horizontal,\n   diagonal), which guarantees the existence of the required redundant lines.\n\n   Choosing any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines (for instance\n   \\(S_j : y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{j}{2}\\;(j=1,\\dots ,k)\\)) and discarding any\n   \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(V_i\\) yields a family with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n   and still covering all points.\n\nThus the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is sharp, and every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and a basic counting lemma  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n*If a line \\(L\\) is sunny then \\(|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le 2\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny then \\(|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  Suppose three distinct\npoints \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lie on such a line.\nThen the three pairwise slopes are equal to \\(m\\).  Because all coordinates are\nintegers bounded by \\(n\\), the only possible common slopes are \\(0\\),\n\\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); this contradicts the definition of sunny.  Hence at most\ntwo points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can lie on a sunny line.\n\nIf \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n*Vertical* (\\(x=c\\)): the constant \\(c\\) can be any integer \\(1,\\dots ,n\\);\nfor a fixed \\(c\\) the admissible \\(y\\)-coordinates satisfy\n\\(1\\le y\\le n+1-c\\), giving at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Horizontal* (\\(y=c\\)): analogous.\n\n*Diagonal* (\\(x+y=c\\)): the constant \\(c\\) can be any integer\n\\(2,\\dots ,n+1\\); for a fixed \\(c\\) the admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates satisfy\n\\(1\\le x\\le c-1\\) and also \\(x\\le n+1-c\\), again giving at most \\(n\\) points.\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering\ncondition and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f1 the total number of\nincidences between the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and the points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n2k + n\\,(n-k).\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be incident to at least one line,\n\\[\n2k + n\\,(n-k) \\;\\ge\\; |\\mathcal P_n|\n      = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\qquad\\text{(by (2)).}\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\\[\n2k + n^2 - nk \\ge \\frac{n^2+n}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk \\le n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A covering family consisting of \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_i &: x=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nH   &: y=1,\\\\\nD   &: x+y=2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll three types are non\u2011sunny (vertical, horizontal, diagonal).  \nWe claim that the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-2},H,D\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof of covering.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have three\npossibilities:\n\n1. **\\(a\\le n-2\\).** Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n2. **\\(a\\ge n-1\\).** Then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le 2\\).  \n   *If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(H\\).  \n   *If \\(b=2\\)*, the point satisfies \\(a+b= a+2\\ge n+1\\); the only way to stay\n   inside \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is \\(a=n-1\\) and \\(b=2\\), which gives\n   \\(a+b= n+1\\).  In this case \\(a+b=2+(n-1)=n+1\\) and the point lies on the\n   diagonal line \\(D\\) (since \\(x+y=2\\) is satisfied only for \\((1,1)\\); the\n   previous sub\u2011case actually never occurs).  \n   A more direct argument: if \\(a\\ge n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  When \\(b=1\\) we are\n   on \\(H\\); when \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the point \\((a,2)\\) satisfies\n   \\(a+2\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n-1\\).  The line \\(D\\) is \\(x+y=2\\) and contains\n   only the point \\((1,1)\\); thus the only remaining points with \\(b=2\\) are\n   covered by a vertical line \\(V_a\\) because now \\(a\\le n-2\\).  Hence every\n   point is covered.\n\nConsequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal F\\).  Moreover each point belongs to **three** lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\):\nthe vertical line \\(V_a\\) (if \\(a\\le n-2\\)), the horizontal line \\(H\\) (if\n\\(b=1\\)), and the diagonal line \\(D\\) (if \\(a+b=2\\)).  In particular each\nvertical line \\(V_i\\) contains at least three points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the\npoints \\((i,1), (i,2),\\dots\\)).  Hence every line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is\n*redundant*: removing a single line still leaves every point incident to at\nleast one of the remaining lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Add sunny lines.**  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines; for concreteness we may take  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j :\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\frac{j}{2}\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes \\(\\frac12\\) differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so each \\(S_j\\) is\n   sunny.  The lines \\(S_j\\) are distinct because they have different\n   intercepts.\n\n2. **Delete redundant lines.**  \n   Because each line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is redundant, we may delete any\n   \\(k\\) of them without destroying the covering property.  For definiteness\n   delete the first \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\).\n\n3. **Resulting family.**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k:=\\bigl(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal F|-k + k = (n-2)+k-k = n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny.\n   Since the deleted vertical lines were redundant, every point of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is still incident to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have produced a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe upper bound (3) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); the construction of\nSection\u202f4 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}.\n\\]"
