            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf e_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf e_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf e_{-1}=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\nThus a **non‑sunny** line is horizontal, vertical, or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  No configuration with exactly one sunny line  \n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\) and that exactly one line, say \\(L_s\\), is sunny.\nConsequently the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them is parallel\nto one of the three forbidden directions.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\]\n\nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\qquad (n\\ge 3). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nObserve the following facts.\n\n*If a point of \\(S\\) lies on a vertical line, that line is \\(x=a\\) with\n\\(a\\ge 2\\); if it lies on a horizontal line, the line is \\(y=b\\) with\n\\(b\\ge 2\\); if it lies on a line of slope \\(-1\\), the sum \\(a+b\\) is constant\nand at least \\(4\\).*\n\nBecause the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines are parallel to at most two of the three\nforbidden directions, at least one of the three families (vertical, horizontal,\nslope \\(-1\\)) is **absent** among them.  We treat the three possibilities\nseparately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  No line of slope \\(-1\\) among the non‑sunny lines  \n\nThen every point of \\(S\\) must be covered by a vertical or a horizontal line.\nThere are at most \\(n-1\\) such lines in total.  \n\nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 2\\) can contain at most \\(n-c\\) points of\n\\(S\\) because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le n+1-c\\).  Similarly a\nhorizontal line \\(y=d\\) with \\(d\\ge 2\\) can contain at most \\(n-d\\) points of\n\\(S\\).  Hence the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by\n\\(n-1\\) vertical/horizontal lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{n}(n-i)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}=|S|.\n\\]\n\nEquality would require **all** vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) (or all\nhorizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\)) to be present, which would already give\n\\(n-1\\) lines.  In that case the remaining line would have to be sunny, but a\nsunny line cannot be vertical or horizontal, contradicting the assumption that\nonly one sunny line exists.  Therefore the covering is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  No vertical line among the non‑sunny lines  \n\nNow every point of \\(S\\) must be covered by a horizontal line or a line of slope\n\\(-1\\).  A horizontal line \\(y=d\\) (\\(d\\ge 2\\)) contains at most \\(n-d\\) points\nof \\(S\\); a line of slope \\(-1\\) with equation \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 4\\)) contains\nexactly one point of \\(S\\) (namely \\((c-2,2)\\) or \\((2,c-2)\\)).  Hence with at\nmost \\(n-1\\) such lines we can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=2}^{n}(n-d)+(n-3)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}=|S|,\n\\]\n\nand the same equality‑case argument as above leads to a contradiction with the\nrequirement that only one line be sunny.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  No horizontal line among the non‑sunny lines  \n\nThis case is symmetric to 2.2 and yields the same contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince in every possible distribution of the three directions at least one\ndirection is missing, we obtain a contradiction.  Hence a configuration with\nexactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so it lies on the line\n\\(x=a\\).  All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none is sunny.\nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  The case \\(2\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns=n-k\\ (\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Sunny lines:}&\\qquad V_j:\\;x=j\\quad (j=1,\\dots ,k),\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{Non‑sunny lines:}&\\qquad D_t:\\;x+y=t+2\\quad (t=0,1,\\dots ,s-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe \\(V_j\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-intercepts, and the \\(D_t\\) have distinct\nconstants \\(t+2\\); none of the \\(V_j\\) coincides with a \\(D_t\\) because a\nvertical line cannot be of slope \\(-1\\).  Hence we have exactly \\(k+s=n\\)\ndifferent lines.\n\n*Sunny property.*  \nEach \\(V_j\\) is vertical, thus not parallel to \\(\\mathbf e_x\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\mathbf e_y\\) (vertical) nor \\(\\mathbf e_{-1}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)); consequently it\nis sunny.  The \\(D_t\\) are parallel to \\(\\mathbf e_{-1}\\) and therefore\nnon‑sunny.\n\n*Covering of \\(T_n\\).*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(V_a\\).\n\n* If \\(a>k\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k+1)=s .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence \\(a+b\\le s+k+1=n+1\\) and \\(a+b-2\\) is an integer between \\(0\\) and\n  \\(s-1\\).  Therefore \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=a+b = (a+b-2)+2,\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. it lies on the line \\(D_{\\,a+b-2}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\nExactly \\(k\\) of the lines (\\(V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\)) are sunny.\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family of\n\\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nNo configuration with exactly one sunny line exists, while configurations\nexist for all other non‑negative integers \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
