"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. any non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   With only \\(n\\) distinct lines we can have at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n   Split the \\(n\\) lines into three groups  \n\n   * \\(h\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\);  \n   * \\(v\\) vertical lines   \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\);  \n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\): \\(y=x+c_j\\) with suitable intercepts \\(c_j\\).\n\n   Choose  \n\n   \\[\n   h=v=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor ,\\qquad \n   r=n-k-2h\\in\\{0,1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and add an extra horizontal line when \\(r=1\\).  \n   Thus the total number of lines is \\(h+v+k=n\\) and exactly the \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(1\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Coverage proof.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\le h\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   *If \\(a\\le v\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *Otherwise* \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=n-h,\n   \\qquad\n   b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(v+1)=n-v .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le a-v\\le n-h-v,\\qquad\n   1\\le b-h\\le n-h-v .\n   \\]\n\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   d:=b-a\\;=\\;(b-h)-(a-v)+(h-v).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le a-v,b-h\\le n-h-v\\), the difference \\((b-h)-(a-v)\\) ranges over all integers from \\(-(n-h-v-1)\\) to \\(n-h-v-1\\). Adding the constant \\(h-v\\) we see that  \n\n   \\[\n   d\\in\\{\\,c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\,\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where we define the \\(k\\) intercepts  \n\n   \\[\n   c_j:=h-v-(k-2j+1),\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,k .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\(b=a+c_j\\) for a unique \\(j\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+c_j\\).\n\n   Thus every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); together with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) this yields the exact set of admissible values.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, and the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nIf we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny. Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k .\n\\]\n\nWe shall use \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal or vertical) and the remaining\n\\(k\\) lines will be sunny (slope \\(1\\)).\n\n#### 2.1. Choice of horizontal and vertical lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Big\\rfloor ,\\qquad \nv:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m\\) is odd we add one extra horizontal line, so that the total number of\nnon\u2011sunny lines is exactly \\(m\\).  \nThus the set of non\u2011sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\,y=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le h\\,\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le v\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nand possibly the line \\(y=h+1\\) when \\(m\\) is odd.  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none\nof them is sunny.\n\n#### 2.2. Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\nDefine, for \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\),\n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + c_j,\\qquad \nc_j:=h-v-(k-2j+1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\); consequently it is not parallel to any of the three\nforbidden directions and is therefore sunny.  \nThe intercepts \\(c_j\\) are distinct, so the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal V|+k = h+v+k = n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 \\(b\\le h\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\(a\\le v\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n\n*Case 3 \u2013 \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\).*  \nBoth inequalities are strict, so we may write  \n\n\\[\na=v+\\alpha,\\qquad b=h+\\beta,\n\\]\n\nwith integers \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha+\\beta = (a-v)+(b-h) \\le n+1-(v+h)=k+1 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\), (3) implies  \n\n\\[\n1\\le\\alpha,\\beta\\le k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nd:=b-a = (h+\\beta)-(v+\\alpha)= (h-v)+(\\beta-\\alpha). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe difference \\(\\beta-\\alpha\\) can take any integer value from \\(-(k-1)\\) to\n\\(k-1\\). Adding the constant \\(h-v\\) we see that the set of all possible\nvalues of \\(d\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,h-v-(k-1),\\;h-v-(k-3),\\dots ,\\;h-v+(k-1)\\,\\}\n =\\{c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\},\n\\]\n\nby the definition (2) of the intercepts. Hence there exists a unique index\n\\(j\\) such that \\(d=c_j\\). Equation (4) then reads \\(b=a+c_j\\), i.e. the point\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Counting sunny lines\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are sunny; all\nother lines are horizontal or vertical and therefore not sunny.  Hence the\nnumber of sunny lines in the configuration equals the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n\\) (inequality (1)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we exhibited an explicit set of\n  \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a\nconfiguration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

