[\nL_j:\\; y = x + c_j,\\qquad \nc_j:=h-v-(k-2j+1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\); consequently it is not parallel to any of the three\nforbidden directions and is therefore sunny. The intercepts \\(c_j\\) are distinct,\nso the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal V|+r+k = h+v+r+k = n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n#### Case 1 \u2013 \\(b\\le h\\) (or \\(b\\le h+1\\) when \\(r=1\\))\n\nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\) (or the extra\nhorizontal line if \\(b=h+1\\)).\n\n#### Case 2 \u2013 \\(a\\le v\\)\n\nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n\n#### Case 3 \u2013 \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\)\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\na=v+\\alpha,\\qquad b=h+\\beta,\n\\]\n\nwith integers \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha+\\beta = (a-v)+(b-h) \\le n+1-(v+h)=k+1 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n1\\le\\alpha,\\beta\\le k .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nd:=b-a = (h+\\beta)-(v+\\alpha)= (h-v)+(\\beta-\\alpha). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem reduces to showing that for every integer\n\\(d\\) of the form \\((h-v)+t\\) with \\(t\\in\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) there exist\n\\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) satisfying (3) and \\(\\beta-\\alpha=t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma (Existence of \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\))\n\nFor any integer \\(t\\) with \\(-(k-1)\\le t\\le k-1\\) there exist integers\n\\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha+\\beta\\le k+1,\\qquad \\beta-\\alpha = t .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha:=\\max\\{1,\\,1-t\\},\\qquad \\beta:=\\alpha+t .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha\\ge1\\) and \\(t\\ge-(k-1)\\), we have \\(\\beta\\ge1\\). Moreover,\n\\(\\alpha\\le k\\) and \\(\\beta\\le k\\) because \\(t\\le k-1\\). Hence \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\le k\\),\nso \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\le 2k\\).  \n\nIf \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\le k+1\\) we are done. Otherwise \\(\\alpha+\\beta = k+1+q\\) with\n\\(q\\ge1\\). Observe that decreasing both \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) by the same\npositive integer \\(q\\) preserves the difference \\(\\beta-\\alpha\\) and reduces the\nsum by \\(2q\\). Choose \\(q:=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\alpha+\\beta-(k+1)}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\).\nThen the new pair  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha'=\\alpha-q,\\qquad \\beta'=\\beta-q\n\\]\n\nstill satisfies \\(\\beta'-\\alpha'=t\\) and now fulfills \\(\\alpha'+\\beta'\\le k+1\\).\nSince \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\le k\\), the subtraction never makes a component drop below\n\\(1\\). Thus such a pair exists. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nReturning to (5), let \\(t:=\\beta-\\alpha\\). By the Lemma we can realise any\n\\(t\\) with \\(-(k-1)\\le t\\le k-1\\). Consequently the set of possible values of\n\\(d=b-a\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\, (h-v)+t \\mid t=-(k-1),\\dots ,k-1 \\,\\}\n   =\\{c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere the equality follows from the definition (2) of the intercepts\n\\(c_j\\). Hence there exists a unique index \\(j\\) such that \\(b-a=c_j\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nb = a + c_j,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Counting sunny lines\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are therefore\nsunny; all other lines are horizontal or vertical and are not sunny. Hence the\nconfiguration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Any configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n\\) (inequality (1)).\n* F