\), we also include the extra horizontal line \\(y=h+1\\).\nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none\nof them is sunny. Their total number is \\(h+v+r=m=n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.2. Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + c_j,\\qquad \nc_j:= (h-v)+t_j,\\qquad \nt_j:=-(k-1)+2(j-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\); consequently it is not parallel to any of the three\nforbidden directions and is therefore sunny. The numbers \\(c_j\\) are distinct,\nso the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal V|+r+k = h+v+r+k = n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n#### Case 1 – \\(b\\le h\\) (or \\(b=h+1\\) when \\(r=1\\))\n\nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\) (or the extra\nhorizontal line if \\(b=h+1\\)).\n\n#### Case 2 – \\(a\\le v\\)\n\nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n\n#### Case 3 – \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\)\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\na=v+\\alpha,\\qquad b=h+\\beta,\n\\]\n\nwith integers \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha+\\beta = (a-v)+(b-h) \\le n+1-(v+h)=k+1 .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n1\\le\\alpha,\\beta\\le k .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\beta-\\alpha .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) and (6) we have the following elementary description of the possible\nvalues of \\(t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1 (possible differences).**  \nFor the integers \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\) satisfying \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\le k+1\\) the\ndifference \\(t=\\beta-\\alpha\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n-(k-1)\\le t\\le k-1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad t\\equiv k-1\\pmod 2 .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConversely, for every integer \\(t\\) obeying (8) there exist \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\)\nwith \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\le k+1\\) and \\(\\beta-\\alpha=t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(s=\\alpha+\\beta\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\)). Then \\(\\alpha=(s-t)/2\\),\n\\(\\beta=(s+t)/2\\). Since \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) are integers, \\(s\\) and \\(t\\) must have\nthe same parity; because \\(s\\) ranges from \\(2\\) to \\(k+1\\), the parity of\n\\(t\\) is forced to be the parity of \\(k-1\\). Moreover \\(|t|\\le s-2\\le k-1\\),\ngiving (8).  \n\nConversely, given a \\(t\\) satisfying (8) set  \n\n\\[\ns:=k+1-|t| \\quad(\\text{so }2\\le s\\le k+1\\text{ and }s\\equiv t\\pmod2).\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(\\alpha=(s-t)/2,\\;\\beta=(s+t)/2\\). Both are positive integers and\n\\(\\alpha+\\beta=s\\le k+1\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom (7) and the Lemma we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb-a = (h+\\beta)-(v+\\alpha) = (h-v)+t = c_j\n\\]\n\nfor a unique index \\(j\\) because the numbers \\(c_j\\) in (4) are precisely the\nvalues \\((h-v)+t\\) with \\(t\\) satisfying (8). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny\nline \\(L_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Counting sunny lines\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) have slope \\(1\\); all other lines are\nhorizontal or vertical, hence not sunny. Therefore the configuration contains\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Any configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n\\) (inequality (3)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set\n  of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a\nconfiguration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
