"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have identified the critical error pointed out in the bug report (the inequality for \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\) was wrong when the number of non\u2011sunny lines is odd). By modifying the distribution of the non\u2011sunny horizontal and vertical lines, the construction becomes valid for every parity of \\(n-k\\). Consequently the complete and rigorous solution is now correct.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   With only \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny, so \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Choice of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   Put  \n\n   \\[\n   h:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Big\\rfloor ,\\qquad \n   v:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(h+v=m\\) for **all** \\(m\\) (both even and odd).  \n   Use the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\) and the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\).  \n   These \\(m\\) lines are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny.\n\n3. **Sunny lines of slope\u202f1.**  \n   For \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\; y = x + c_j,\\qquad \n   c_j:= (h-v)+t_j,\\qquad \n   t_j:=-(k-1)+2(j-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The intercepts \\(c_j\\) are distinct, so the \\(k\\) lines are distinct and all have slope\u202f1; hence they are sunny.\n\n4. **Coverage of the lattice set**  \n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we consider three cases.\n\n   *If \\(b\\le h\\) or \\(a\\le v\\)* the point lies on a horizontal or vertical line, respectively.\n\n   *Otherwise* write \\(a=v+\\alpha,\\;b=h+\\beta\\) with \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha+\\beta = (a-v)+(b-h) \\le n+1-(v+h)=n+1-m = k+1. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (Because \\(v+h=m\\) by construction, the right\u2011hand side is always \\(k+1\\).)\n\n   Set \\(t:=\\beta-\\alpha\\).  \n   Using \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\) and (1) one proves the elementary\n\n   **Lemma 1.**  \n   The integers \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\) satisfying \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\le k+1\\) give a difference  \n\n   \\[\n   -(k-1)\\le t\\le k-1,\\qquad t\\equiv k-1\\pmod 2. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Conversely, for every integer \\(t\\) obeying (2) there exist such \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\).\n\n   Because the intercepts \\(c_j\\) in the definition of the sunny lines are exactly the numbers \\((h-v)+t\\) with \\(t\\) satisfying (2), we have \\(b-a=(h-v)+t=c_j\\) for a unique \\(j\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\).\n\n5. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_j\\) have slope\u202f1, all other lines are horizontal or vertical, so the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), and the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible. Therefore the admissible values are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nand call a line **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nIf we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny, because a\nline is either sunny or not. Hence any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k .\n\\]\n\nWe shall use \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal or vertical) and the remaining\n\\(k\\) lines will be sunny (slope\u202f1).\n\n#### 2.1. Horizontal and vertical lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nh:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Big\\rfloor ,\\qquad \nv:=\\Big\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nh+v=m \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad h\\le v\\le h+1 .\n\\]\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\,y=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le h\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nand the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le v\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to the coordinate axes, therefore none of them is\nsunny. Their total number is \\(h+v=m=n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.2. Sunny lines (slope\u202f1)\n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + c_j,\\qquad \nc_j:= (h-v)+t_j,\\qquad \nt_j:=-(k-1)+2(j-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\); consequently it is not parallel to any of the three\nforbidden directions and is sunny. The numbers \\(c_j\\) are distinct because the\n\\(t_j\\) form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(2\\). Hence the \\(k\\)\nlines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\nThe whole family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal V|+k = h+v+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n#### Case 1 \u2013 \\(b\\le h\\)\n\nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).\n\n#### Case 2 \u2013 \\(a\\le v\\)\n\nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n\n#### Case 3 \u2013 \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\)\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\na=v+\\alpha,\\qquad b=h+\\beta,\n\\]\n\nwith integers \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\).  \nFrom the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\alpha+\\beta = (a-v)+(b-h) \\le n+1-(v+h)=n+1-m = k+1. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The equality \\(v+h=m\\) holds by the definition of \\(h\\) and \\(v\\); therefore the\nright\u2011hand side is exactly \\(k+1\\) for **all** parities of \\(m\\).)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\beta-\\alpha . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (possible differences)\n\nLet \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\) satisfy \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\le k+1\\). Then  \n\n\\[\n-(k-1)\\le t\\le k-1,\\qquad t\\equiv k-1\\pmod 2,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t=\\beta-\\alpha\\). Conversely, for every integer \\(t\\) obeying the two\nconditions there exist \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\ge1\\) with \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\le k+1\\) and\n\\(\\beta-\\alpha=t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(s=\\alpha+\\beta\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\)). Then  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\frac{s-t}{2},\\qquad \\beta=\\frac{s+t}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) are integers, \\(s\\) and \\(t\\) must have the same parity.\nBecause \\(s\\) can be any integer between \\(2\\) and \\(k+1\\), the parity of \\(t\\)\nmust be the same as that of \\(k-1\\). Moreover \\(|t|\\le s-2\\le k-1\\), giving the\nbounds.  \n\nConversely, given a \\(t\\) satisfying the two conditions, set  \n\n\\[\ns:=k+1-|t|\\quad(\\text{so }2\\le s\\le k+1\\text{ and }s\\equiv t\\pmod2).\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(\\alpha=(s-t)/2,\\;\\beta=(s+t)/2\\); they are positive integers and\n\\(\\alpha+\\beta=s\\le k+1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have  \n\n\\[\nt\\in\\{-(k-1),-(k-3),\\dots ,k-1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(t\\) runs through all integers with the same parity as \\(k-1\\) and lying\nbetween \\(-(k-1)\\) and \\(k-1\\). By the definition (4) of the sunny lines,\nthe set of intercepts \\(\\{c_j\\}_{j=1}^k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(h-v)+t\\mid t\\text{ satisfies (2)}\\}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nb-a = (h+\\beta)-(v+\\alpha) = (h-v)+t = c_j\n\\]\n\nfor a unique index \\(j\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Number of sunny lines\n\nThe only lines with slope different from \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) are the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\), all of which have slope \\(1\\). Consequently they are sunny,\nwhile all horizontal and vertical lines are not sunny. Hence the configuration\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n\\) (inequality (3)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the construction above provides\n  \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a\nconfiguration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
